# 🚚 Delivery Delay Prediction

An end-to-end Machine Learning regression project that predicts food delivery time using Linear Regression, helping businesses improve delivery planning, operational efficiency, and customer satisfaction.

---

## 🎯 Problem Statement and Business Impact

Late deliveries are a major challenge for businesses operating in food delivery, e-commerce, and logistics. Unexpected delays can negatively affect customer satisfaction, increase operational costs, and reduce business efficiency.

This project uses Linear Regression to predict the estimated delivery time based on delivery-related factors.

### Business Objectives

- Predict estimated delivery time for individual orders.
- Help businesses improve delivery planning.
- Identify factors influencing delivery duration.
- Improve operational efficiency and resource allocation.
- Support data-driven decision-making in logistics operations.

### Who Benefits?

| Stakeholder | Business Benefit |
|---|---|
| Logistics Managers | Better delivery planning |
| Delivery Companies | Improved operational efficiency |
| Customers | More reliable delivery expectations |
| Business Analysts | Data-driven delivery insights |

---

## 📊 Dataset

The project uses a food delivery dataset containing delivery-related information such as distance, weather, traffic, vehicle type, preparation time, and courier experience.

### Dataset Information

| Attribute | Description |
|---|---|
| Dataset Name | `Delivery_Data.csv` |
| Source | https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/denkuznetz/food-delivery-time-prediction |
| Number of Rows | 1,000 |
| Number of Columns | 9 |
| Target Variable | `Delivery_Time_min` |
| ML Task | Regression |
| Model | Linear Regression |

### Dataset Features

| Feature | Description |
|---|---|
| `Order_ID` | Unique identifier for each delivery |
| `Distance_km` | Distance between the restaurant and customer |
| `Weather` | Weather conditions during delivery |
| `Traffic_Level` | Traffic conditions during delivery |
| `Time_of_Day` | Time period of the delivery |
| `Vehicle_Type` | Vehicle used for delivery |
| `Preparation_Time_min` | Time taken to prepare the order |
| `Courier_Experience_yrs` | Courier's years of experience |
| `Delivery_Time_min` | Target variable representing delivery time |

### Target Variable

The model predicts `Delivery_Time_min`, a continuous numerical variable representing the time required to complete a delivery.

---

## ⚙️ Approach

The project follows an end-to-end Machine Learning workflow.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Dataset Collection] --> B[Exploratory Data Analysis]
    B --> C[Data Cleaning]
    C --> D[Feature Engineering]
    D --> E[One-Hot Encoding]
    E --> F[Train-Test Split]
    F --> G[Feature Scaling if Required]
    G --> H[Linear Regression]
    H --> I[Model Evaluation]
    I --> J[Save Model and Preprocessing Objects]
    J --> K[Streamlit Application]
    K --> L[Delivery Time Prediction]
```

### Machine Learning Pipeline

1. **Data Collection:** Load the delivery dataset using Pandas.
2. **Exploratory Data Analysis:** Analyze feature distributions, relationships, and correlations.
3. **Data Cleaning:** Handle missing values, duplicates, and inconsistencies.
4. **Feature Engineering:** Prepare relevant features for model training.
5. **Encoding:** Convert categorical variables into numerical representations using One-Hot Encoding.
6. **Train-Test Split:** Divide the dataset into training and testing sets.
7. **Model Training:** Train a Linear Regression model.
8. **Model Evaluation:** Evaluate predictions using regression metrics.
9. **Model Serialization:** Save the trained model and preprocessing objects.
10. **Deployment:** Integrate the model into an interactive Streamlit application.

---

## 📈 Results

The Linear Regression model was trained to estimate delivery time using delivery-related features.

### Model Evaluation

| Model | MAE | MSE | RMSE | R² Score |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Linear Regression | — | — | — | — |

### Evaluation Metrics

- **MAE (Mean Absolute Error):** Average absolute difference between actual and predicted delivery times.
- **MSE (Mean Squared Error):** Average squared prediction error.
- **RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error):** Prediction error expressed in the same unit as delivery time.
- **R² Score:** Measures how much variation in delivery time is explained by the model.

The model's performance is evaluated using unseen test data to assess its ability to predict delivery times for new orders.

